{
 "_what": "Expected verdict envelope for the 22 golden labels (ARCHITECTURE §11.9). tests/test_golden.py asserts each image lands inside `allowed` and never in `forbidden`.",
 "_derivation": [
  "Every entry starts from spike/corpus/ground_truth.json — NEVER from what the tool currently outputs, or the test would prove only that the code does what it does.",
  "Mapping: warning_status == 'compliant' AND expected_record_match  ->  allowed {green, amber}, forbidden {red}.",
  "Mapping: any violation status, OR expected_record_match == false  ->  allowed {red, amber}, forbidden {green}.",
  "Carve-out for warning_status == 'v_tiny': the violation on those labels is type SIZE, which §1 and §15.1 place outside what this tool measures — 16.22(b) is not verifiable from an uncalibrated photograph, so it is reported on the informational panel and never enters the roll-up. Their text, capitalisation and bold are compliant, so the honest envelope is allowed {green, amber}, forbidden {red}. Forbidding green on them was a derivation error: it demanded the tool reject a label for something it does not claim to measure.",
  "`derived_allowed` records the status-only mapping's output for every entry — before the tightenings that narrow it and before the v_tiny carve-out that widens it.",
  "basis='tightened': one of the eight cases in §11.9 where the corpus is unambiguous and the tool must be exactly right.",
  "basis='known_hard': one of the three cases in §11.9 that are expected to land amber, documented rather than quietly dropped.",
  "The 22 ground-truth rows are copied in below because spike/corpus/ is git-ignored: the golden suite must run in a fresh clone against app/samples/.",
  "Keys are the corpus filenames. app/samples/ re-encodes these to JPEG (§8.4), so the test matches on the filename STEM, not the extension."
 ],
